{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The {{Nihongo|'''Honda-Nakayama Cabinet'''|本田・中山内閣|Honda-Nakayama Naikaku}}, officially the 109th Cabinet of Okaiken, was the executive government of Okaiken from 5 November 2025 to 17 May 2026. Formed following the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]], it was a coalition between the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party (PPO)]] and the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP)]], with external support from the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Social Democratic Party (SDP)]] and the [[Okaiken Innovation Party|Okaiken Innovation Party (OIP)]] on a vote-by-vote basis.

The cabinet was the first coalition government in Okaiken since the Aoyagi administration and was characterised by deep ideological tensions between the PPO's centrist fiscal conservatism and the OSP's democratic socialist platform. It collapsed on 12 March 2026 following the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] and the subsequent "[[March Maelstrom]]" civil unrest, when the OSP withdrew from the coalition and the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] passed a motion of no confidence. The cabinet continued in a caretaker capacity until the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 general election]] on 17 May 2026.

==Formation==
{{main|Okaiken general election, 2025}}
The 2025 general election produced a fragmented Diet in which no single party commanded an absolute majority of 238 seats. The PPO, under Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]], lost 20 seats to finish with 181, while the OSP under [[Rento Nakayama]] surged from 2 to 50 seats in a populist wave dubbed "{{Nihongo|Nakayama-mania|中山フィーバー|Nakayama Fībā}}." The LDP under [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] gained 40 seats to reach 128.

Following intense negotiations, Honda and Nakayama announced a formal coalition on 4 November 2025. The PPO-OSP bloc held 231 seats—seven short of an absolute majority—necessitating a fragile governance style that required seeking additional support from minor parties on a vote-by-vote basis. Nakayama was appointed Minister of Health and Welfare, giving him oversight of the implementation of Honda's signature [[National Universal Health Act]].

The cabinet was formally sworn in on 5 November 2025. It comprised fourteen PPO ministers and two OSP ministers, reflecting the PPO's dominant position within the coalition.

